{
  "id": "dw8qy72",
  "title": "Chapter 1: Chemical Reactions and Equations",
  "order": 21,
  "blocks": [
    {
      "id": "23pmhu7",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "1. Physical and Chemical Changes"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Chemical reactions produce new substances with entirely distinct physical and chemical properties.",
        "Balanced chemical equations maintain an equal count of each element's atoms on both reactant and product sides to satisfy the Law of Conservation of Mass.",
        "Chemical reactions are broadly classified into Combination, Decomposition, Displacement, Double Displacement, and Redox reactions.",
        "Exothermic reactions release energy into the surroundings, while endothermic reactions absorb thermal energy.",
        "Corrosion damages metal surfaces via oxidation by atmospheric gases, whereas rancidity degrades fats and oils in food."
      ],
      "id": "zfenpmt"
    },
    {
      "type": "statStrip",
      "stats": [
        {
          "label": "Key Law",
          "value": "Conservation of Mass",
