    {
      "id": "xym00pd",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "2. Chemical Reactions"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "2x32tjr",
      "content": "A chemical reaction is a process in which reactants are converted into products with formation of new substances."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "1hxhf2h",
      "content": "<strong>Indicators of Chemical Reaction:</strong>"
    },
    {
      "id": "v9r13bf",
      "type": "list",
      "items": [
        " Change in colour",
        "Evolution of gas",
        " Change in temperature",
        "Formation of precipitate",
        "Change in state"
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "j6zdau3",
      "content": "Example:"
    },
    {
      "id": "frs668x",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "3. Balanced Chemical Equation"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "cto3eao",
      "content": "According to the Law of Conservation of Mass, mass can neither be created nor destroyed. The number of atoms of each element must be equal on both sides of a chemical equation."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "obbn62z",
      "content": "Example: "
    },
    {
      "id": "iuc5fmz",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "Types of Chemical Reactions"
    },
    {
      "id": "hpcyihk",
      "type": "table",
      "rows": [
        {
          "isHeader": true,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Type</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Definition</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Example</strong></p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Combination</p>",
            "<p>Two or more substances combine to form one product</p>",
            "<p>CaO + H2O → Ca(OH)2</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Decomposition</p>",
            "<p>One compound breaks into simpler substances</p>",
            "<p>CaCO3 → CaO + CO2</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Displacement</p>",
            "<p>More reactive element replaces less reactive element</p>",
            "<p>Fe + CuSO4 → FeSO4 + Cu</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Double Displacement</p>",
            "<p>Exchange of ions between two compounds</p>",
            "<p>Na2SO4 + BaCl2 → BaSO4 + 2NaCl</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Neutralisation</p>",
            "<p>Acid + Base → Salt + Water</p>",
            "<p>HCl + NaOH → NaCl + H2O</p>"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "bplb6q4",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "Oxidation and Reduction (Redox Reactions)"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "w93uzdo",
      "content": "Oxidation: Addition of oxygen or removal of hydrogen or loss of electrons."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "lzg94bp",
      "content": "Reduction: Removal of oxygen or addition of hydrogen or gain of electrons."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "f5i8730",
      "content": "Example: Zn + CuSO4 → ZnSO4 + Cu"
    },
    {
      "id": "m9duotq",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "Corrosion and Rancidity"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "juk96v8",
      "content": "Corrosion: Slow destruction of metals due to reaction with air and moisture. Example: Rusting of iron."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "9b2613j",
      "content": "Rancidity: Oxidation of oils and fats causing bad smell and taste."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "pwgchk4",
      "content": "Prevention: Use antioxidants, refrigeration, airtight containers."
    },
    {
